{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak… and say.</b> He doubled the expression <b>speak and say</b>, and also changed the language by using two distinct terms, not saying אמור ואמרת — because HaShem commanded two categories of offering: one that comes as a freewill offering — vowed or voluntary — and one for atonement of sin, כי יחטא אדם. Corresponding to this He said <b>דבר</b> ('speak'), for it is a royal decree upon him — the obligation of the designated sin-offering in every detail. And corresponding to that which comes as a freewill offering He said <b>ואמרת אליהם</b> ('and say to them') — this is only for the sake of elevation and honor, and there is no obligation in the matter. Further, it also alludes to the detail of the sin of inadvertencies, in which there is דיבור ('speaking') — representing the aspect of obligation, and אמירה ('saying') — which is because HaShem is pleased to accept an animal in place of the soul of the sinner, for she truly deserves to die, but HaShem redeems a person's soul with a sheep of the flock or a goat, and for one who brings much, an ox of the herd." "And His saying <b>the children of Israel</b> etc. — in Torat Kohanim they said: 'אל בני ישראל' — to exclude gentiles from the semichah (leaning) mentioned immediately nearby. 'בני' ('sons') — and not daughters. 'ואמרת אליהם' — for the semichah mentioned at the end, which is the semichah of shelamim (peace-offerings). And it is difficult: after they derived in the nearby passage an inclusion from לאמר, then 'דבר אל בני ישראל' is needed for its own meaning. And one cannot say that it is automatically understood from the word לאמר, as the author of Korb Aharon explained — with apologies to his honor — for if He had not said 'דבר אל בני ישראל,' I would not have derived what I derived from 'לאמר,' so what room is there to derive from 'דבר' etc.?" "Or perhaps, since it is not known which of them is needed for its own meaning and which of them comes for derivation, we derive from both of them. Or it may be that the Tanna does not derive 'דבר,' but rather 'בני ישראל' — for He should have said 'דבר אליהם,' and the matter would be understood that He is speaking about Israel, and from His saying 'בני' he excludes women, for He should have said 'דבר אל בית ישראל,' and from His saying 'ישראל' he excludes the gentiles. And the first interpretation is primary, for you will find that there in Torat Kohanim he derives 'בני ישראל' to say: just as the children of Israel are parties to the covenant — and through that he establishes his derivation that he derives from the verse 'אדם כי יקריב מכם' — see, he derives many derivations all together as they are heard as one; so too he derives the superfluous 'לאמר' since we can say that this is the superfluous one, and he returns and derives 'דבר אל' etc. since we can say this is the superfluous one." "<b>When a person brings…</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say the word <b>אדם</b> ('person' / 'man'). And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim ad loc.) to include converts. And the master, the author of Korb Aharon, raised the difficulty: why was it necessary to include converts, since it is established for us (Menachot 73b) that we accept offerings from gentiles etc.? And he proposed that one might have thought to exclude them since they entered the category of Israel — and we find that Scripture excludes some of Israel, as it is written 'מכם' ('from among you') and not all of you; therefore it was necessary to include them — end of quotation. But his words, of blessed memory, are not precise at all, for if this inclusion of converts did not exist, I would not have derived from the inclusion of 'איש איש' (Leviticus 22:18) to include gentiles but rather to include converts. Therefore the Tanna preceded and informed us that converts are included from the superfluity of 'אדם'; if so, then 'איש איש' comes to include gentiles. And with this you will proceed on the straight path in the meaning of that entire baraita without any strained difficulty." "And one cannot object that perhaps 'אדם' comes to exclude gentiles (Bava Metzia 114a), as they said: You are called אדם and the nations of the world are not called אדם — and 'איש איש' is to include converts. For from His saying 'דבר אל בני ישראל,' behold, Scripture speaks only of Israel, so how would it enter one's mind to think about accepting from gentiles that one would need to exclude them? Rather, it certainly comes only to include, and we have nothing to include except one category of those who are not children of Israel — and they are converts. And when another inclusion comes, then we will understand that it includes gentiles. And the inclusion of converts — all agree that we accept every type of offering. But the inclusion of gentiles — they disagreed (Menachot 73b), Rabbi Yosi HaGelili and Rabbi Akiva, whether specifically burnt-offerings etc." "<b>And in the Midrash</b> of Tanchuma they said: Why did it say 'adam' and not say 'ish'? It intends to say: when a man (adam) sins — like the first man (adam) who began to sin — he shall bring a sacrifice. Thus far. Now, from the implication of the words of the Midrash, it is implied that the first man was inadvertent, since one brings a sacrifice only for an inadvertent sin. And this aligns with what I explained in parashat Bereshit on the verse (Genesis 3:12) ״הִיא נָתְנָה לִּי״ etc. And the intent of the Midrash is that HaShem came to conduct Himself with the children of Israel in a manner He did not conduct Himself with the first man, for the first man sinned and the sentence of death was decreed upon him, and bringing a sacrifice did not avail him — but for them it would avail. And their saying that he 'began to sin' gives the reason why HaShem did not conduct Himself thus with the first man: for Adam began to sin, and there preceded him no dimension of evil to compel him to sin — unlike those who came after him, for whom the dimension of sin already preceded in their souls. And this is for you the sign of the covenant — that he is foreskinned in his flesh — and HaShem commanded circumcision, which was not so for the first man." "And along this path I succeeded in settling one inquiry: that He judged Adam for his first sin with the sentence of death, and did not show regard for His attribute, blessed be He, as it is written (Job 33:29) ״פַּעֲמַיִם שָׁלֹשׁ עִם גָּבֶר״ — and on the contrary, the rule of the public allows Him to pass over for a person even a third offense, as it is written (Amos 2:1) ״עַל שְׁלֹשָׁ פִּשְׁעֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. For there is a correct reason in the matter: on the day HaShem created man, He established him in the dimension of good without dross — which is the dimension of evil that bends man from the path of good — and through his sin the dimension of evil was drawn down and became a barrier upon every soul of an Israelite: in his soul, his spirit, and his breath, and also in his body — foreskinned of heart and foreskinned in flesh. And therefore if a man of Israel sins, HaShem passes over for him two, three, etc., on account of the portion of evil cleaving to him — evil since he was formed from his mother's womb — which overpowers him to cause him to sin willfully, and also causes him to sin in a moment of distraction. And therefore a sacrifice avails him for his inadvertence. Not so the first man: even though he sinned only inadvertently, it is a just sentence for him that HaShem should not pass over for him even one sin; and even though it was inadvertent, a sacrifice would not avail him for his inadvertence." "<b>When he offers from among you.</b> One must know why it was necessary to say <b>from among you.</b> Further, why did it delay saying ״מִכֶּם״ until after mention of the action proceeding from it — for it would be fitting to place first mention of the subject [the one] through whom He would speak, and afterward the object proceeding from it. And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Ḥullin 5a) said that it comes to exclude the apostate among Israel, and they expounded ״מִן הַבְּהֵמָה״ to include the apostate. How so? It excludes an apostate for the entire Torah or for idolatry, which is weighted against the entire Torah, and includes an apostate who is not [apostate] for the entire Torah. And according to their words of blessed memory, it appears possible to settle also the matter of why it delayed saying ״מִכֶּם״: for if it had said 'adam mikem' it would have been heard as excluding in a place where it includes, or including in a place where it excludes — but such is not the intent of the verse, for the inclusion applies to converts and the exclusion applies to Israel alone. Therefore it separated between them with the word ״כִּי יַקְרִיב.״ And it further appears possible to explain according to what they said in Ḥullin (there 5b), that they brought this baraita which expounded ״מִכֶּם״ to exclude the apostate, and after it they brought a baraita that expounded ״מֵעַם הָאָרֶץ״ — excluding the apostate; Rabbi Shimon says: one who returns from his [former] way. And they said there: [regarding] an apostate to eat fat who brought a sacrifice for the blood — there is a difference between them. And they said there: one [baraita] speaks of a sin-offering and one of a burnt-offering. Thus far." "And they said of blessed memory in Torat Kohanim: ״כִּי יַקְרִיב״ is nothing but voluntary, and this is what was precisely said ״כִּי יַקְרִיב״ before saying ״מִכֶּם״ — to say that what it excludes is in the realm of a vow and a freewill offering. And according to the words of the Gemara (Ḥullin 5a) that concludes: one [teaching] concerns a burnt-offering — lest you say since it is a gift it should be accepted, [the verse] teaches us [otherwise]; and one concerns a sin-offering. And the exclusion required for a sin-offering — behold, it is inscribed in true writing in His saying (Leviticus 4:27) ״מֵעַם הָאָרֶץ,״ and this exclusion differs from the exclusion stated regarding a burnt-offering, for the exclusion stated regarding a burnt-offering applies only to an apostate for the entire Torah, but the exclusion stated regarding a sin-offering excludes even an apostate for a single transgression, if it be that [transgression] for which he brings a sacrifice for its inadvertence. And so it also follows from the words of the Rambam in chapter 3 of Hilkhot Ma'aseh haKorbanot — see there." "<b>And by way of</b> remez (hint), it alludes along this path: because they saw that HaShem brought Moses to Him and called to him from among them all, and performed honor and greatness for Moses — therefore it said <b>adam — when he offers</b> — so much drawing-near to the Shekhinah: this is nothing but <b>from among you</b> — meaning, from the side of Israel, for through their mediation is the attainment [achieved]. And as we wrote above, that HaShem would not speak with him except for the sake of Israel. And go out and learn: for thirty-eight years, during which Israel was rebuked [stricken], the Glory distanced itself from upon him and Elohim did not speak with him." "It further alludes to commanding men of valor to strive to bring the hearts of the people of Israel close to the service of HaShem, and therefore it is called ״קָרְבָּן לַה׳״ — for through a person's sin the attachment of Israel with their Father in heaven is sundered, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים בַּה׳״, and they become separated and distanced from the Shekhinah, and the Master blessed is He is particular, as it were, about this matter and desires to bring them close to Him, and He commanded to reprove every distant person and to bring his heart near. He also punished those who avert their eyes from this matter, and for you this is the sign that HaShem commanded to begin from His hallowed ones and magnified the reward of one who merits others, as the Tanna said (Avot 5:21): all who merit the masses — no sin comes through their hand — for HaShem will guard them from errors, and these are the matters spoken of here." "<b>Adam.</b> It is precise to say ״אָדָם״ in the sense of importance, as stated in the Zohar (Tazria 48a): ״כִּי יַקְרִיב״ — and whom will he bring close? Scripture explained and said <b>מִכֶּם</b> — from the lowliest that are among you, who transgressed against HaShem and said to El ״סוּר מִמֶּנּוּ״ and distanced themselves from attachment to Him blessed be He; this one you shall bring close. And therefore it is called קָרְבָּן לַה׳, for he shall bring close a shoot of His plantings, holy to its root (Isaiah 60:21). And such a person has no need to bring either a vow-offering or a freewill-offering, nor is there any occasion to bring him a sin-offering or a guilt-offering, in accordance with their saying of blessed memory (Avot ch. 5): all who merit the masses — no sin comes through their hand — and if there is no inadvertent sin, there is no offering. And after He finished speaking of the offering of the exalted and superior kind, He said <b>מִן הַבְּהֵמָה וְגוֹ׳ תַּקְרִיבוּ אֶת קָרְבַּנְכֶם</b> — this matter exists for every person, for not all attain the level of one who turns back many from iniquity."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "'Korb Aharon' is the commentary of R. Aaron ben Joseph HaLevi on Torat Kohanim; the phrase במחילה מכבודו is the Or HaChaim's respectful disclaimer before disputing him.", 5 "The wordplay on adam ('man') and Adam haRishon ('the first man') is central to the midrash being cited; the translation preserves the repeated term.", 6 "The phrase ״פַּעֲמַיִם שָׁלֹשׁ״ (literally 'twice, three times') is kept in Hebrew as Scripture; the sense is that God gives multiple warnings/opportunities before punishing.", 9 "רמז ('hint') left untranslated as the technical term for the third level of PaRDeS exegesis; the thirty-eight years refer to the period of Israel's wandering after the sin of the spies."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:53:35.305318634Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}